<section class="demo" id="demo">
    <h2>Try it - the emulator running in your browser</h2>
    <p class="muted">
        The complete emulator (6510, VIC-II, CIAs, SID, optionally the 1541) is compiled to WebAssembly and runs
        client-side; nothing is sent to a server. Click <em>Start</em>, then click the screen to give it keyboard focus.
        Press <em>Run demo</em> for the built-in raster/sprite/SID demo, or drop a .d64 / .t64 / .prg / .crt on the screen.
    </p>
    <Emulator />
</section>

<section class="help" id="controls">
    <h2>Keyboard &amp; controls</h2>
    <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card">
            <h3>Keyboard</h3>
            <p>Keys are mapped by physical position (W3C <code>KeyboardEvent.code</code>), so the layout of your
                keyboard does not matter for letters and digits.</p>
            <table class="keys">
                <tr><th>PC</th><th>C64</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Esc</td><td>RUN/STOP</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Tab</td><td>C= (Commodore)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Ctrl</td><td>CTRL</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Backspace / Delete</td><td>INST/DEL</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Insert</td><td>SHIFT + INST/DEL</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Home / End</td><td>CLR/HOME / SHIFT + CLR/HOME</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Cursor keys</td><td>CRSR (SHIFT added for up/left)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>F1 - F8</td><td>F1 - F8</td></tr>
                <tr><td>` (backquote)</td><td>&#8592; (left arrow)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>[ ]</td><td>@@ *</td></tr>
                <tr><td>' (quote)</td><td>:</td></tr>
                <tr><td>\ or &lt; (intl)</td><td>&#163; (pound)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>= / -</td><td>+ / -</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Page Up</td><td>RESTORE</td></tr>
            </table>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
            <h3>Joystick and host keys</h3>
            <table class="keys">
                <tr><th>PC</th><th>Action</th></tr>
                <tr><td>Numpad 8 / 2 / 4 / 6</td><td>Joystick 2 up / down / left / right</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Numpad 0 / 5, right Alt</td><td>Joystick 2 fire</td></tr>
                <tr><td>F11</td><td>Reset</td></tr>
                <tr><td>F12</td><td>Screenshot (PNG download)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Pause</td><td>Pause / resume</td></tr>
            </table>
            <p>On phones and tablets the <em>Touch controls</em> panel provides a joystick (port selectable),
                RUN/STOP, RETURN, SPACE, the function keys and RESTORE; the <em>Type text</em> box types through the
                KERNAL keyboard buffer, so you can paste BASIC programs.</p>
            <h3>Media</h3>
            <p><em>Open file…</em> or drag &amp; drop attaches <code>.d64</code> disks (needs the 1541 ROM, autostarts
                <code>LOAD"*",8,1</code> + <code>RUN</code>), <code>.t64</code>/<code>.prg</code> programs (injected into
                RAM and started) and <code>.crt</code> cartridges. <em>Warp</em> runs as fast as your machine allows
                with audio off, handy while a disk loads.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="help" id="roms-help">
    <h2>About the ROMs</h2>
    <div class="card">
        <p>A C64 needs three ROM images (BASIC, KERNAL, character generator) and the 1541 needs its DOS ROM. Commodore's
            originals are still under copyright, so this site does not ship them. Instead it boots
            <a href="https://github.com/MEGA65/open-roms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenROMs</a>
            (MEGA65 project, LGPL-3.0): a clean-room, free re-implementation of BASIC, KERNAL and character set that boots
            to a READY prompt, runs BASIC and machine code and is enough for the built-in demo and most cartridges. It is
            not byte-compatible with the originals, so some software that jumps into ROM routines will not run on it, and
            there is no free 1541 DOS - disk images therefore need the real drive ROM.</p>
        <p>If you own the original ROMs, open <em>Load your own ROMs</em> in the emulator and pick the files
            (<code>basic.901226-01.bin</code>, <code>kernal.901227-03.bin</code>, <code>characters.901225-01.bin</code>,
            <code>1541-II.251968-03.bin</code> or the <code>c000</code>/<code>e000</code> halves, or the combined
            <code>64c.251913-01.bin</code>). They are kept in your browser's localStorage only and never uploaded.</p>
        <p class="muted small">Developers: <code>tools/fetch-openroms.ps1</code> / <code>.sh</code> download OpenROMs into
            <code>wwwroot/roms</code>; the files are git-ignored.</p>
    </div>
</section>
